Changes:
 ros-jazzy-unitree-description (1.2.0-2026.08.25.10.25) noble; urgency=high
 .
   * Apply per-joint encoder zero offsets in the G1 hardware interface
     Robot #1 <https://github.com/qiayuanl/unitree_ros2/issues/1>'s arm encoders do not read zero when the arm is physically at
     zero: each of the twelve arm joints is off by a measured constant, the
     largest being left shoulder roll at 4.04 deg. Correcting them takes
     open-loop hand-to-hand accuracy from 38.3 mm rms to 6.5 mm.
     UnitreeSdk2 applies the offset symmetrically, so everything above the
     hardware interface keeps working in physical coordinates and stays
     unaware of the encoder frame.
     Velocity, effort and the PD gains are untouched -- a constant offset
     leaves dq and tau invariant, and PD closes on the motor in encoder frame,
     so a shifted q_target is just a shifted equilibrium. Applying only one
     half would step the joint by the offset the instant a controller
     activates, with gains already live.
     perform_command_mode_switch deliberately needs no change: it latches
     posDes_ from pos_, and the two are in the same frame, so write() sends
     back exactly the raw encoder reading and command equals measurement at
     activation, whenever the switch happens to be scheduled. Folding the
     offset into pos_ itself, rather than an extra field, is what keeps that
     true.
     Note the URDF <limit> tags stay in encoder units, so a corrected joint
     can read slightly outside them; enabling enforce_command_limits would
     need those bounds shifted too.
     The offsets reach the plugin as per-joint <param> tags emitted by the
     shared joint_interface macro and filled from a YAML at xacro time, so
     they end up in /robot_description and therefore in every bag. Joints the
     file omits default to 0.0. The file is chosen per run by the
     joint_offsets_file argument, empty by default, so an unspecified run and
     simulation -- already in physical coordinates -- behave exactly as before.
     The offsets file itself is generated calibration rather than source: it is
     named per robot and invalidated by re-zeroing, so it is not carried in the
     repo or the installed share tree. Point joint_offsets_file wherever the
     robot's file actually lives.
     A file that is not a mapping is rejected by xacro, an offset beyond
     0.2 rad is rejected at init as a unit or sign error, and the number of
     offsets applied is logged unconditionally, so a forgotten or partially
     written file is visible rather than silent.
     Launch files select the file with the joint_offsets_file argument, whose
     declaration and path handling live in the new unitree_description.joint_offsets
     module so each consumer needs only an import and two call sites.
     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <mailto:noreply@anthropic.com>
